@Ecore(nsURI="https://expression.models.nasdanika.org", nsPrefix="org.nasdanika.models.expression")
@GenModel(
    modelDirectory="/model/src-gen",
    featureDelegation="Dynamic",
    complianceLevel="25",
    suppressGenModelAnnotations="false",
    copyrightFields="false",
    operationReflection="true",
    importOrganizing="true"
)
/*
 * DRAFT. A closed, minimal algebra of boolean expressions over bound values,
 * intended to be PUSHED DOWN to a data source.
 *
 * Ships with core, because it is foundational: the graph content provider
 * contract is written in terms of it, and the Cypher engine, the rules
 * engine and any future provider all speak it.
 *
 * WHAT THIS IS NOT
 *
 * It is not an expression language and it is not the Cypher expression AST.
 * The Cypher AST is faithful to its source: round-trippable, markered, tolerant
 * of fragments it cannot parse, sixty-odd classes. This model is the opposite,
 * and deliberately so: a provider author must be able to switch exhaustively
 * over it in an afternoon. Cypher LOWERS into this model; the lowering is where
 * "what can be pushed down here" is decided.
 *
 * THE GOVERNING RULE
 *
 * A construct belongs here only if a data source could plausibly evaluate it
 * for itself. Everything else stays residual and the caller applies it after
 * the fetch. That rule is what keeps this at twenty classifiers, and it is safe
 * to apply strictly because THE FALLBACK IS ALWAYS CORRECT: an expression that
 * cannot be pushed is simply evaluated by the caller. Expressiveness here buys
 * performance, never correctness, so there is never an urgent reason to grow it.
 *
 * So: no arithmetic, no function calls, no collection comprehensions, no
 * user-defined operators. Those arrive as OpaqueExpression and are applied by
 * whoever asked. See the readme for the full list of deliberate omissions.
 *
 * SYNTAX ONLY, SEMANTICS SUPPLIED BY THE APPLICATION
 *
 * This is the load-bearing boundary. Cypher's three-valued null logic, its
 * ordering across mixed types and its integer/float coercions are TCK-specified
 * and are NOT general truths. If they were baked in here, every other consumer
 * of this model would inherit Cypher's semantics and Cypher would still have to
 * be checked against the TCK.
 *
 * Therefore: the generated evaluate() implements NEUTRAL (Java-like) semantics
 * and exists for convenience. An application with its own semantics walks the
 * tree with its own visitor and does not call it. Cypher will do exactly that.
 */
package org.nasdanika.models.expression

annotation "http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/Ecore" as Ecore
annotation "http://www.eclipse.org/emf/2002/GenModel" as GenModel
annotation "urn:org.nasdanika" as Nasdanika

type Class<T> wraps java.lang.Class
type Map<K, V> wraps java.util.Map
type Instant wraps java.time.Instant
type CompletionStage<T> wraps java.util.concurrent.CompletionStage

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Evaluation
 *
 * Evaluator is defined HERE rather than in nxcore, and nxcore refines it:
 *
 *     interface Evaluator extends expression.Evaluator, Documented, Marked {}
 *
 * which is the same re-declaration-under-the-same-name move the role floor
 * already makes with Content and Section. The reason is dependency direction:
 * core's graph module has to evaluate a predicate and must not drag
 * documentation-as-structure to do it. Evaluating is more primitive than
 * documenting.
 *
 * The consequence to be deliberate about: a bare Expression is NOT Marked, so an
 * expression lowered from Cypher source carries no line and column unless the
 * lowering produces nxcore-refined variants. Markers on expressions are what make
 * a provider's rejection reportable against source text, so losing them silently
 * would be a real regression in diagnostics. See the assessment for the three
 * options and the recommendation.
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/*
 * Something that produces a value from bindings. The synchronous face, and the
 * primary one.
 *
 * Primary because the dominant case is in-memory and CPU-bound: a residual
 * predicate applied once per row. Making that path allocate a CompletionStage per
 * row to accommodate the rare evaluator that does IO is the wrong trade. Note
 * that this is the OPPOSITE orientation from the graph content provider, where
 * IO dominates and async is primary. Same pattern, inverted for the same reason:
 * each face is primary where its case is dominant.
 */
interface Evaluator {

    op <T> T evaluate(Class<T> resultType, Map<String, Object> bindings)

    op <T> T evaluate(Class<T> resultType) {
        return evaluate(resultType, null)
    }

    op Object evaluate(Map<String, Object> bindings) {
        return evaluate(null, bindings)
    }

    op Object evaluate() {
        return evaluate(null, null)
    }

    /*
     * What evaluating this costs, declared rather than discovered.
     *
     * A caller needs to know STATICALLY whether a conjunction can be applied
     * synchronously, because a single IO or INFERENCE operand makes the whole
     * conjunction async. It is also what lets a scope policy refuse a class of
     * evaluator outright, which is how "no inference in this answer" becomes
     * enforceable rather than conventional.
     */
    op EvaluationCost cost() {
        return EvaluationCost.LOCAL
    }

}

/*
 * Ordinal rather than a number, because absolute costs are unknowable and every
 * consumer only needs ordering: refuse above a threshold, evaluate cheapest first.
 */
enum EvaluationCost {
    /* A literal or a field read. */
    FREE = 0
    /* In-memory computation. The default. */
    LOCAL = 1
    /* Touches a file, a socket or a remote service. */
    IO = 2
    /* Invokes a model. Non-deterministic, and the one a policy most often refuses. */
    INFERENCE = 3
}

/*
 * The asynchronous face, for evaluators that genuinely do IO or inference.
 * Adapters exist both ways and an implementation provides whichever is natural.
 *
 * CompletionStage here, Mono in the graph content provider, and the inconsistency
 * is deliberate. Two reasons. This is a single deferred value with no stream to
 * pair with, which is exactly what CompletionStage is for, whereas the provider
 * returns metadata plus a stream and wants Mono and Flux together. And this is a
 * MODEL rather than a Java SPI: it has to stay portable enough to project to
 * TypeScript, so a Reactor type in a generated EMF class is a commitment that a
 * plain interface in core does not make. Composing the two is one call to
 * Mono.fromCompletionStage, which is acceptable friction for keeping a core model
 * free of anything but the JDK.
 */
interface AsyncEvaluator {

    op <T> CompletionStage<T> evaluateAsync(Class<T> resultType, Map<String, Object> bindings)

    op <T> CompletionStage<T> evaluateAsync(Class<T> resultType) {
        return evaluateAsync(resultType, null)
    }

    op EvaluationCost cost() {
        return EvaluationCost.IO
    }

}

/*
 * Base of the algebra.
 *
 * Extends Evaluator, which is the move that makes the whole contract uniform: a
 * caller holds a list of predicates, some of them structured Expressions it can
 * inspect and offer to a provider, others opaque Evaluators (Groovy, SpEL, a
 * lambda) that can only be run. Both are Evaluator, so the residual-application
 * path is one line and does not care which it got.
 *
 * NOT Marked and not a ModelElement, which is the price of sitting below nxcore.
 * Expressions do not need identity, uris or an icon, and twenty classifiers each
 * carrying six unused features is how a micro-model stops being micro. The cost
 * is source markers, which a Cypher lowering genuinely wants; the assessment
 * recommends recovering them through a Marked mixin in the nxcore-aware
 * companion rather than by pulling nxcore down here.
 */
abstract class Expression extends Evaluator {

    /*
     * Neutral evaluation, for convenience. See the package comment: an
     * application with its own semantics uses its own visitor instead. Declared
     * once here so every subclass inherits one implementation.
     */
    @GenModel(body="return org.nasdanika.models.expression.util.ExpressionSupport.evaluate(this, resultType, bindings);")
    op <T> T evaluate(Class<T> resultType, Map<String, Object> bindings)

}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Literals
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/*
 * A constant. Split by type rather than carried as a single Object value,
 * because a provider has to switch on the type to build its own query, and
 * because an Object-valued attribute does not survive serialization to JSON
 * without a side channel saying what it was.
 *
 * Integer and float are separate, not merged into "number", because Cypher
 * distinguishes them and so do most of the targets. Merging here would force
 * every consumer to re-derive the distinction from the value.
 */
abstract class Literal extends Expression {}

class StringLiteral extends Literal {
    String value
}

/* 64-bit signed. */
class IntegerLiteral extends Literal {
    long value
}

/* Double precision. */
class FloatLiteral extends Literal {
    double value
}

class BooleanLiteral extends Literal {
    boolean value
}

/*
 * Explicit null. A distinct class rather than an unset value, so that "compare
 * against null" is visibly different from "this operand was never populated",
 * which is a bug.
 */
class NullLiteral extends Literal {}

/*
 * An instant. The one non-primitive literal, because range predicates over time
 * are the single most commonly pushed-down filter in every API this will meet:
 * updated since, created before, modified between.
 */
class TemporalLiteral extends Literal {
    Instant value
}

/*
 * An ordered list of expressions. Exists for InTest's right-hand side and for
 * nothing else in this version, which is why it is not called ListLiteral: its
 * elements need not be constant.
 */
class ListExpression extends Expression {
    contains Expression[] elements
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * References
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/*
 * The whole bound value, by name. In a query this is a pattern variable; in a
 * rule it is whatever the caller bound.
 */
class VariableReference extends Expression {
    String variable
}

/*
 * A property of a bound value: i.status, r.defaultBranch.
 *
 * This is the class pushdown is actually about. A provider that can filter at
 * all can almost certainly filter on a named property of the thing it produces,
 * and a provider that cannot recognize this class can push nothing.
 */
class PropertyReference extends Expression {
    String variable
    String property
}

/*
 * A named parameter, resolved from bindings at evaluation time: $status.
 *
 * Worth having as a class rather than inlining constants at lowering time for
 * two reasons. A provider can often bind a parameter into a prepared remote
 * query, and a logged or cached expression keeps its shape without carrying the
 * value, which matters when the value is a credential or a customer identifier.
 */
class ParameterReference extends Expression {
    String name
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Predicates
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

enum ComparisonOperator {
    EQUAL as "=" = 0
    NOT_EQUAL as "<>" = 1
    LESS as "<" = 2
    LESS_OR_EQUAL as "<=" = 3
    GREATER as ">" = 4
    GREATER_OR_EQUAL as ">=" = 5
}

class Comparison extends Expression {
    ComparisonOperator operator
    contains Expression left
    contains Expression right
}

enum LogicalOperator {
    AND = 0
    OR = 1
}

/*
 * N-ary rather than binary, and this is not cosmetic. Pushdown negotiation is
 * conducted over a CONJUNCTION: the caller offers a list of operands and the
 * provider reports which of them it honored. A binary tree would force every
 * provider to flatten it first, and every provider would flatten it slightly
 * differently.
 */
class LogicalOperation extends Expression {
    LogicalOperator operator
    contains Expression[] operands
}

/*
 * Negation. The only way to negate anything in this model: the individual
 * predicates below carry no "negated" flag, so there is exactly one spelling of
 * NOT and a provider has one case to handle rather than five.
 *
 * Lowering should push negation toward the leaves where it can (negation normal
 * form), because most sources can express "not equal" and very few can express
 * "not (a and b)".
 */
class Not extends Expression {
    contains Expression operand
}

/*
 * IS NULL. Separate from Comparison against NullLiteral because in most target
 * languages, and in Cypher, equality with null is not a null test.
 */
class NullTest extends Expression {
    contains Expression operand
}

/* Membership. The collection is usually a ListExpression or a ParameterReference. */
class InTest extends Expression {
    contains Expression value
    contains Expression collection
}

enum StringTestKind {
    STARTS_WITH = 0
    ENDS_WITH = 1
    CONTAINS = 2
    /* Regular expression, in the java.util.regex dialect. Rarely pushable. */
    MATCHES = 3
}

class StringTest extends Expression {
    StringTestKind kind
    contains Expression value
    contains Expression pattern
}

/*
 * Type test: is this value an instance of this type, by name.
 *
 * First class rather than encoded as a property comparison, because in this
 * ecosystem the type system is IN the graph: a node reaches its EClass by an
 * eClass edge and its supertypes by eSuperTypes edges. A provider that knows its
 * own types can answer "only repositories" without fetching anything else, which
 * is the cheapest and most common filter there is.
 *
 * typeName is matched by the application against whatever it calls a type:
 * an EClass name for EMF, a label for a property graph, a resource kind for an
 * API. includeSubTypes distinguishes "exactly this" from "this or below", which
 * every source spells differently and none of them leave implicit.
 */
class TypeTest extends Expression {
    contains Expression operand
    String typeName
    boolean includeSubTypes = "true"
}

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * The escape hatch
 * --------------------------------------------------------------------------- */

/*
 * Anything this model cannot express: arithmetic, a function call, a script, a
 * Cypher list comprehension.
 *
 * It is an Expression so it composes inside LogicalOperation and travels with
 * the rest of the tree, and it delegates to any Evaluator (a Groovy or SpEL
 * evaluator, a compiled lambda) so the caller can still run it. It is never pushable, by construction, and a provider that
 * meets one reports it as not honored without needing to know what it was.
 *
 * This class is what makes the governing rule affordable. Refusing to grow the
 * algebra costs nothing when everything refused still has somewhere to go.
 */
class OpaqueExpression extends Expression {
    /*
     * What to run. Contained rather than referenced, so the expression stays
     * self-contained when it is serialized or cached.
     */
    contains Evaluator delegate
    /*
     * Optional human-readable source, for diagnostics and for EXPLAIN output.
     * Never parsed.
     */
    String description
}
